<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">1755 Lisbon earthquake</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1755">1755</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 1755 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> earthquake was a catastrophic earthquake, followed by a tsunami and fires, that destroyed much of the Portuguese capital in 1755. It killed tens of thousands of people and devastated the city's buildings, archives and commercial wealth. In the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> it is treated as a blow that deepened the kingdom's decline, although the reconstruction led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquis-of-pombal">Marquis of Pombal</a> also prompted major administrative reform.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/1755-lisbon-earthquake.jpg" alt="1755 Lisbon earthquake" width="320" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: USGS · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1755_Lisbon_Earthquake_Location.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The earthquake struck at about 09:40 local time on Saturday, 1 November 1755, the Christian feast of All Saints. Seismologists estimate its magnitude at 7.7 or greater, with an epicentre in the Atlantic about 200 km west-southwest of Cape St. Vincent. Contemporary reports give a duration of three and a half to six minutes and describe fissures 5 metres wide in the city centre. Survivors fled to the docks and watched the sea recede; about 40 minutes later a tsunami engulfed the harbour and downtown area, followed by two more waves. Candles lit for the feast were knocked over, and the fires grew into a firestorm that burned for hours.</p><p>Damage was widespread beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>. Most coastal towns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algarve">Algarve</a> were heavily damaged, except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faro">Faro</a>, which was sheltered by the sandy banks of the Ria Formosa. Funchal on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madeira">Madeira</a> and the ports of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azores">Azores</a> suffered, and the tsunami struck Cadiz and the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. Waves reached Martinique and Barbados, and a three-metre wave hit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>. Economic historian Álvaro Pereira estimated that of Lisbon's population of about 200,000, between 30,000 and 40,000 died, with perhaps another 10,000 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>About 85 per cent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>'s buildings were destroyed, among them the Royal Ribeira Palace, whose 70,000-volume library, paintings and royal archives were lost, along with records of the voyages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> and other early navigators. King Joseph I and the court had left the city after Mass and survived. The prime minister, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquis-of-pombal">Sebastião José de Carvalho e Melo</a>, 1st Marquis of Pombal, is reported to have answered the question of what to do with the words &quot;Bury the dead and heal the living&quot;. Many corpses were buried at sea against the wishes of the Church, the army was deployed, and more than thirty people were publicly executed as looters.</p><p>On 4 December 1755 the chief engineer Manuel da Maia presented four plans, from abandoning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> to building a new city. The king and Pombal chose the last, razing the Baixa quarter and laying out rectilinear streets; the Pombaline buildings are among the earliest seismically protected constructions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Pombal also sent a questionnaire on the shock, the sea and the fires to every parish, and the priests' answers, still kept in the Torre do Tombo, have led him to be seen as a forerunner of modern seismology. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> drew on the disaster in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candide">Candide</a> and a poem, attacking the idea that this is the best of all possible worlds, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immanuel-kant">Immanuel Kant</a> published three texts on it in 1756.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “1755 Lisbon earthquake” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Brazilian Gold Rush, sparked by the discovery of gold in Minas Gerais in the 1690s, and the discovery of diamonds around 1729 produced a second revenue surge that funded João V’s lavish court and, later, helped pay for the rebuilding of Lisbon after the <b>1755 Lisbon earthquake</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>1755 Lisbon earthquake</b>, which struck on 1 November, shook optimistic faith in a benevolent order of the world; Voltaire answered it with a poem in 1756 and with his satire Candide in 1759.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>…school system was later copied abroad; Joseph II freed peasants from personal serfdom and granted toleration to Protestants and Jews; Charles III of Spain (1759–1788) promoted science, trade and agriculture; and in Portugal the Marquis of Pombal directed the rebuilding of the capital after the <b>1755 Lisbon earthquake</b>.</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · Strengths</a></li></ul></section>
